# 22) Explain dependency injection and inversion of control

IoC is the principle; DI is one way to implement that principle.
In simple Hindi:
- IoC (Inversion of Control): Object banane aur manage karne ka control application code se Spring container ko dena.

Simple definition: Inversion of Control means the responsibility for creating objects and managing their lifecycle is transferred from your application code to a framework or container.
In simple Hindi: Normally, hum apne code mein new keyword se objects banate hain. IoC mein object banane aur manage karne ki responsibility Spring Framework leta hai.


- DI (Dependency Injection): Jo object kisi class ko chahiye, us object ko class ke andar khud create karne ke bajay bahar se provide karna.

Simple definition: Dependency Injection is a technique in which an object receives the other objects it needs from outside rather than creating them itself.
For example, OrderService needs PaymentService to process an order. Spring injects that dependency through the constructor.

4. Types of Dependency Injection in Spring


1. Constructor Injection — Recommended
public OrderService(PaymentService paymentService) {
    this.paymentService = paymentService;
}


Best for mandatory dependencies. Supports final fields, makes dependencies explicit, and simplifies unit testing.



2. Setter Injection
@Autowired
public void setPaymentService(
        PaymentService paymentService) {
    this.paymentService = paymentService;
}


Useful for optional or changeable dependencies, though changing dependencies after initialization needs care.


3. Field Injection
@Autowired
private PaymentService paymentService;

Easy to write, but generally discouraged in production code because dependencies are hidden and isolated unit testing becomes harder.

Note: When a Spring bean has a single constructor, @Autowired is generally unnecessary on that constructor.

Note: When a Spring bean has a single constructor, @Autowired is generally unnecessary on that constructor.
5. Real-world production scenario
Imagine you have an e-commerce application with 30 microservices.
Your OrderService needs to send notifications after an order is placed.
A tightly coupled implementation might be:
public class OrderService {
    private final EmailNotificationService notification =
            new EmailNotificationService();
}


Now, if you want to switch from email notifications to a Kafka-based notification publisher, you may need to modify the order service.
A better design uses an interface:
public interface NotificationService {
    void sendNotification(String message);
}


@Service
public class EmailNotificationService
        implements NotificationService {

    @Override
    public void sendNotification(String message) {
        System.out.println("Email: " + message);
    }
}


@Service
public class OrderService {

    private final NotificationService notificationService;

    public OrderService(NotificationService notificationService) {
        this.notificationService = notificationService;
    }

    public void placeOrder() {
        // Save order and perform business logic
        notificationService.sendNotification("Order placed");
    }
}


Spring injects an implementation of NotificationService. If you register multiple implementations, you must resolve the choice using @Qualifier, @Primary, or another explicit configuration.
In a real distributed production system, sending notifications through Kafka would usually be implemented as a messaging adapter or event publisher rather than treating a remote microservice as an ordinary in-process Java object.
Production benefits:
- Loose coupling between classes.
- Easier unit testing with mocks.
- Easier replacement of implementations.
- Better maintainability and separation of concerns.
6. Common senior-level follow-up: What happens if multiple beans implement the same interface?
Suppose you have both EmailNotificationService and SmsNotificationService implementing NotificationService.
Spring may throw NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException if it cannot determine which bean to inject.
You can resolve it with @Qualifier:
public OrderService(
        @Qualifier("emailNotificationService")
        NotificationService notificationService) {
    this.notificationService = notificationService;
}


Or use @Primary on the default implementation.
